**Grant Abstract – Community Literacy Advancement Initiative (CLAI)**  

The Community Literacy Advancement Initiative (CLAI) will deliver a comprehensive, data‑driven literacy program to the River‑Stone Metro District, employing a multi‑modal instructional framework that guarantees a spectrum of quantifiable outcomes. Over the 24‑month project period, CLAI will:  

- Enroll **500 adult learners** in foundational reading classes and **300 adult learners** in advanced literacy workshops;  
- Recruit **1 200 children (ages 5‑12)** for weekly story‑time circles and **800 pre‑teens (ages 13‑15)** for youth‑led book clubs;  
- Distribute **4 500 new‑edition books** (including multilingual titles) to households, schools, and community centers;  
- Achieve **≥ 90 % attendance** at all scheduled sessions, measured by weekly roll‑call sheets;  
- Attain a **75 % course‑completion rate** among adult participants and a **85 % retention rate** for youth groups;  
- Raise reading‑level assessment scores of adult learners by **≥ 30 %** (pre‑/post‑test comparison) and of children by **≥ 25 %** on state‑standardized literacy benchmarks;  
- Conduct **150 parent‑family literacy workshops**, with **≥ 80 %** reporting increased confidence in supporting children’s reading;  
- Facilitate **200 community‑wide reading festivals** and **300 inter‑generational story‑exchange events**, each tracked by participant‑count logs;  
- Train **50 local volunteers** as certified literacy mentors, maintaining **≥ 95 %** certification‑exam pass rates;  
- Deploy **12 mobile literacy vans** delivering services to **30 underserved neighborhoods**, logging **≥ 5 000 service‑hours**;  
- Collect **≥ 5 000 digital interaction points** (e‑book downloads, online tutoring minutes) via a dedicated literacy app;  
- Publish **12 quarterly impact reports** featuring disaggregated data on demographic reach, skill gains, and community‑economic indicators;  
- Secure **≥ 80 %** post‑program employment or continued‑education placement for adult graduates, verified through partner‑agency surveys;  
- Record a **10 % reduction** in school‑age absenteeism in participating neighborhoods, as reported by district attendance records;  
- Measure a **15 % increase** in household‑wide reading frequency (≥ 3 books/month) through pre‑/post‑program household surveys.  

Each outcome is tracked with rigorous quantitative metrics, ensuring transparent evaluation and maximal impact amplification for every dollar invested in CLAI.

